[Music]  let's talk about  money  when was the last time you told someone  how much you make  how much is in your bank account  how about how you invest your capital if  you invest at all  would you be willing to tell me  right now  my bet  probably not  because that's what society has taught  us isn't it  at the dinner table we don't discuss  religion  politics  or money  well  let me tell you why i wish we would  talk about money  flashback to freshman fall  i had just finished moving in and i'm  meeting the guys who live down the hall  nick and matt  and nick  kicks off the intros  now at first it actually seems like nick  and i have a lot in common  he's six feet tall  i'm  five ten and three quarters but it's  basically the same thing  he's in the sailing team and i mean i  don't want to brag but i was captain of  the jp volleyball team back in ninth  grade  but then he says his dad  works at a hedge fund  uh what's a hedge fund  matt chimes in and says that his dad  also works in finance he's a banker  oh well that one i know like a bank  teller that's cool so i ask him which  bank switch matt responds  goldman  huh  never heard of goldman must be some tiny  bank  personally i'm a wells fargo boy  then they ask  how about you kevin what do your folks  do  oh they're both waiters mom's been the  sheridan since before i was born  nick raises an eyebrow  whoa wait are you  on financial aid  yeah i lucked out and got a ton you guys  and without answering me nick and matt  exchange a look  change the topic  and move on  what just happened  why are they so surprised  is it weird that i'm on financial aid  should i be ashamed that my parents are  waiters  maybe i should say they work in  hospitality  or maybe i should avoid talking about  them all together  these unwelcome thoughts plagued me  during my time at harvard as i continued  to meet peers who were shocked almost  uncomfortable to hear by my background  making me feel like i didn't belong  so  to combat this growing insecurity  i decided to pursue the most lucrative  career path i could think of  investment banking  so  fast forward a few years i'm now 22  years old working on wall street making  more than my parents combined  and that's not a flex  it's the reality  i'm chatting with my buddy rob and he's  complaining about the bank's no trade  policy  but as someone who had only learned  about the stock market through econ  courses in college i ask  why do you care  do you even own stock  yeah of course my mom bought me some  apple shares when i was 10 and i started  trading back in high school don't you  uh no  but that's cool could you show me how to  get started sometime  i don't know about that man you should  just ask your financial advisor  who the heck has a financial advisor at  20  while i didn't even know how to open a  brokerage account  rob has been earning passive income  before he learned long division  what  so at this point i'm trying to feel  pretty frustrated i went to the same  college as rob i worked at the same firm  as rob i even had the same job as rob so  i thought the playing field was leveled  why was i still so behind  well  the reality was  as the son  of blue-collar immigrant parents who  didn't go to college  and as a product of boston's public  school system  i was financially illiterate  just like 90 percent of high school  graduates in this country  here's some stats  the average american makes around fifty  thousand dollars a year  nearly two thirds of americans live  paycheck to paycheck  and the majority of americans  can't afford a 500 emergency expense  think about that one  unexpected accident and they fall into  debt  but why does this matter  why should you care  well  it's because financial literacy is  directly linked  to wealth inequality  in the u.s the top 10 of households hold  70 percent of this country's wealth  while the bottom 50 percent  hold  two  this massive wealth divide is  unsustainable and it puts our society at  risk of instability  but to bring it down to a more personal  level  it also results in limited opportunity  and mobility for the disadvantaged  for people like my mother  who had the same job as a waitress her  entire working life  and put every penny she earned into a  low interest savings account because she  didn't know any  better had she developed financial  fluency sooner  she wouldn't have torn her shoulder from  carrying heavy platters for 30 years  straight  and today  she wouldn't be depending upon the  checks i sent her monthly as her main  source of income since she can no longer  work from her injuries  here at the gsb  one in ten students identify as first  generation low-income  meaning there are others in this room  whose family share similar stories  if we want a fair and equal society we  must reduce this inequality  and we need to recognize that we all can  help narrow this gap  so here's what i'd like to see more of  recently i was catching up with my  former boss  he's a cfo and has become a life mentor  and close friend  now as someone quickly approaching 30  i'm starting to think about financially  preparing for  the wedding  the house  children  also known as money parasites  so i really needed his advice  and when i told him my net worth  unprompted  he was taken aback  kevin are you usually this open with  your personal  finances yeah why not  huh okay  well i think you're off to a good start  but here are some things i'd start  thinking about  and he starts rattling on about  increasing my exposure to crypto 529  plans backdoor roth conversions  irrevocable trusts  let me just stop there who in this room  just heard complete gibberish  my point exactly  these are tools and strategies to  diversify and grow my wealth and now i  feel much better prepared to start the  next chapter of my life  but recognize that this only happened  because of two things  i was willing to be transparent  ask questions share my numbers  and i found someone who was willing to  share their knowledge  so here's my call to action for you  let's normalize talking about money  for those of you who feel like you  struggle with personal finances there's  no need to feel embarrassed your net  worth does not dictate your personal  worth as a human being  it takes a lot of courage to embrace  your financial situation but allow  yourself to be vulnerable and seek out  advice  now for those of you who are more  financially savvy  share your knowledge  while i appreciate the intent to be  modest that humility ends up being  harmful because it prevents the exchange  of valuable information  recognize that sharing your knowledge is  infinitely more helpful than not  and if we can all start doing this  we will take a massive step forward  in addressing the financial knowledge  gap we have in this country  and my hope  is that as a result we can tackle wealth  inequality from a grassroots perspective  and together build a more trusting  financially aware society  and in this world  maybe people like me  don't have to face the same insecurity i  did growing up  more importantly  maybe people like my mother will get the  opportunities that they deserve  all we have to do  is talk about  money  thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

